      "id": "test-acl-workspace",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "CCNA",
      "topic": "Virtual IPv4 access policy",
      "unit": "",
      "title": "Development ACL configuration fixture",
      "summary": "Unbundled backend/native development fixture, not new curriculum or objective coverage.",
      "objective": "Allow OfficePC to reach Portal on TCP port 443 and UDP port 53. Block TCP port 22 as it enters Gateway through office. Edit Gateway’s configuration, keep the supplied network unchanged, and check your work. Either an explicit deny rule or the implicit deny is valid.",
      "retry_guidance": "Check the first matching rule, protocol and destination port, then the selected virtual interface direction. Required allowed traffic must still work.",
      "difficulty": "Beginner",
      "minutes": 20,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Edit the complete virtual router document",
        "Check allowed and denied service flows"
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "explanation": "A populated inbound ACL can allow the required TCP/UDP services while blocking SSH. The first matching rule decides each stated flow; omitted matches use implicit deny. The no-ACL baseline proves routing still works independently of filtering.",
      "hints": [
        "A populated list has implicit deny; an unattached interface does not filter in this exercise.",
        "The packet arrives at Gateway through office. Use the inbound direction at that virtual label.",
        "Destination-port matching comes after the destination address; source-port matching is a separate position."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/routers/ios/config/17-x/sec-vpn/b-security-vpn/m_sec-create-ip-apply-0.html",
        "https://learningcontent.cisco.com/documents/marketing/exam-topics/200-301-CCNA-v1.1.pdf"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Unbundled development fixture only. Bounded native configuration documents and typed packet metadata; no real IOS session, hardware ports, host commands, real packets, TCP connection state, NAT, IPv6 or arbitrary service verification. Internal acl-author action data is authoring-only and never learner Cisco/Linux syntax. Checked sources2026-10-23; no coverage promotion. Only the stated virtual topology and service flows are verified, not arbitrary hosts or ports. office/service are virtual interface labels; the editor is not full IOS CLI.",
